  "notes": "Measured from HP-9814CMP (737-8, 2026-01-12, left side); HP-9932CMP (737-9, 2026) and HP-1730CMP (737-800, from below, 2025) agree. The globe's arcs are simplified to seven bands: four white, three gold. The fin's globe is drawn as areas, so no separate tail logo. Small type markings ('737 MAX 8' under the cockpit) are left out. Copa has worn this Continental-era globe scheme since around 2000 (date not confirmed here). Its Colombian sister Copa Colombia (later Wingo) wore it too; Wingo now has its own scheme. Re-checked 2026-11-15 on HP-9814CMP (737-8, left, 2026-01-12), measured between its doors and placed by the window line: the title runs x 0.198-0.372, its baseline just inside the windows' top (y ~0.2) and its capitals 0.41 tall (it had been drawn smaller and higher, at y 0.39, cap 0.3); lowercase 0.70 of the capitals. The globe's arcs on the fin are curves (smooth). The 737-800s and MAX 9s carry the title at the same place behind the forward door.",
  "history": "New: Copa Airlines wasn't in Skyarium v1's table."
}
